ies  in brief the main causes are  corruption  severe economic mismanagement  political incompetence and arrogance and  misguided policies you know like the um  overnight uh chemical fertilizer ban  last year um which is why uh in addition  to all these crises um i mean we also  have a a looming food crisis to look  forward to and you know currently you  know i'm uh i'm experiencing power cut  right now so uh  this is the state of things or state of  affairs right now in sri lanka  you've touched on it a bit there these  these stories of desperation and  suffering which is sort of veering into  a humanitarian crisis how is this  situation affecting people  yeah i mean you're right undeniably it  is a humanitarian crisis  there's a shortage of life-saving drugs  and medical equipment  the majority of small businesses have  completely closed down  you know even well-established  businesses  informal workers and  daily wage earners are undergoing  immense suffering um you know because of  skyrocketing inflation and as human  rights activists have pointed out in  addition to a humanitarian crisis this  has also turned into a human rights  crisis you know just a few hours ago uh  peaceful protesters were arrested and  i'm getting reports that even uh uh  you know about 20 minutes back uh  protesters were tear gassed right so uh  and just a few days ago we had uh a  footage of an army officer kicking a  citizen at a fuel ship and this man was  not even a protester you know he was  just simply asking some questions so  over the and we see this kind of uh  incidents over the last few uh uh you  know of the last few weeks um or you  know of police police brutality and  armed forces brutality so it is a  humanitarian crisis as well as a human  rights crisis right now a human rights  crisis too people of course want to  weigh out the imf and the world bank  though are hesitant to come to the  rescue  of sri lanka why is that  well the prime minister actually  mentioned in parliament yesterday uh  that the first round of discussions with  the imf has been a success and  but the assistance depends on a debt  restructuring program  now the imf recently stressed that other  challenges are needed to be addressed  like  containing rising inflation  attending to the severe balance of  payments pressures  and uh reducing the country's  vulnerability to corruption so in in my  opinion there is a correlation between  the imf and political stability you know  dealing with corruption having a proper  plan um and assurance that the  government will stick to the conditions  of the of the imf deal once it's agreed  so these are some other challenges i  think uh uh which we are facing uh when  it comes to the agreement uh with imf  lots of challenges that you've outlined  there people in sri lanka say this this  situation why the country is in this  situation is the fault of the powerful  and dominant rajapaksa family why are  they getting the  blame well uh the rajapaksa family has  been often labeled uh as a family  business and if you go back to uh  rajabaksha's presidential campaign in  2019  a kind of a you know i like a highly  mythicized figure was created you know  the the the  political slogan was badakarana  which means the war hero that works so  the waters were very optimistic of a  systems change but then it was evident  that the family just picked up where  they left off in 2015 anger started  building up and which eventually  developed into the current slogans which  is gohan quota and go home rajapaksas um  i think people now see that the  decisions that were taken by the  president votable rajabaksha uh pm and  finance finance minister uh mahindra  rajapaksa and then basil rajasthan who  was also a finance minister for a short  time  have led to the current prices so this  is why the the people are blaming the  raja doctor  there are some big regional players here  of course that that being india and  china what have they promised or or